<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!--
    Without a declared theme, Android fell back to the system default (with an ActionBar) — the
    platform itself drew a native bar with the app label ("Even") on top of the real UI,
    duplicating the `TopAppBar` each Compose screen already draws (found while porting the
    prototype's theme toggle: every TopAppBar got a sun/moon button, and the ghost native bar
    would have been left floating on top). NoActionBar removes that native bar.

    The colours below are the only ones NOT drawn by Compose, and they are the same `paper` token
    (`@color/even_paper`, see res/values[-night]/colors.xml) that `EvenTheme` paints the screens
    with: the cold-start window and the API 31+ splash, which exist before the first composition,
    plus the system bars. Without them the launch window flashes the platform's default grey/black
    and the app visibly "snaps" into its own background. Everything else still comes from
    `EvenTheme` — never from here.

    `enableEdgeToEdge()` in MainActivity makes the real bars transparent at runtime and picks the
    icon contrast from the night mode; `statusBarColor`/`navigationBarColor` are the pre-Compose
    fallback, and `windowLightStatusBar` keeps the launch window's icons dark on light paper.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Even" parent="android:Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/even_paper</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@color/even_paper</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@color/even_paper</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar" tools:targetApi="27">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenBackground" tools:targetApi="31">@color/even_paper</item>
    </style>
</resources>
